## Error propagation through middleware composition

There are two layers: per-middleware catching inside `compose()`, and a final catch-all in `hono-base.ts#dispatch()`.

### 1. Inside `compose()` — `src/compose.ts`, `dispatch(i)`

Each call to `dispatch(i)` wraps its handler invocation in try/catch:

```ts
try {
  res = await handler(context, () => dispatch(i + 1))
} catch (err) {
  if (err instanceof Error && onError) {
    context.error = err
    res = await onError(err, context)
    isError = true
  } else {
    throw err
  }
}
```

Key points:
- `onError` is the **same single reference** (the app's `errorHandler`) closed over by every recursive `dispatch(i)` call — not a fresh per-layer handler.
- Because `dispatch(i+1)` is invoked and awaited *inside* `dispatch(i)`'s own try block (via the `next()` callback passed to `handler`), an error thrown by a deeper/downstream middleware is caught by the **nearest enclosing `dispatch(i)` that directly called the throwing handler**, not necessarily by the outermost layer. Concretely: if middleware C (innermost) throws, `dispatch(indexOfC)`'s catch fires first, invokes `onError(err, context)`, sets `context.res`, and **returns normally** — so the outer middlewares that did `await next()` see a resolved value, not a thrown exception, and continue executing any code after their `next()` call as if nothing failed.
- If `onError` is not set, or the thrown value isn't an `Error` instance, the catch rethrows, so the exception keeps propagating up through each enclosing `dispatch` frame (all of which re-check the same falsy condition) until it escapes `compose()`'s returned promise entirely.
- `isError = true` causes `context.res` to be overwritten even if `context.finalized` was already `true`, ensuring the error response wins over any previously finalized response.

### 2. Outer catch — `src/hono-base.ts`, `#dispatch()`

```ts
const composed = compose(matchResult[0], this.errorHandler, this.#notFoundHandler)
return (async () => {
  try {
    const context = await composed(c)
    if (!context.finalized) {
      throw new Error('Context is not finalized...')
    }
    return context.res
  } catch (err) {
    return this.#handleError(err, c)
  }
})()
```

- `this.errorHandler` (set via `.onError()`, default defined at module scope in `hono-base.ts`) is passed straight into `compose()`, so it's the same function used at every `dispatch` layer described above.
- If an error escapes `compose()` anyway (no `onError` was set, or a non-`Error` was thrown, or the "not finalized" error triggers), it's caught here by `#handleError`:
  ```ts
  #handleError(err: unknown, c: Context<E>): Response | Promise<Response> {
    if (err instanceof Error) {
      return this.errorHandler(err, c)
    }
    throw err
  }
  ```
  This calls the same `errorHandler` a second time as a final safety net, or rethrows non-`Error` values (these propagate out of `.fetch()`/`.request()` entirely).
- The **single-handler fast path** (`matchResult[0].length === 1`, skipping `compose()`) has its own parallel try/catch/`.catch()` around the direct handler call, also routing into `#handleError` — so error handling is duplicated for that optimization but converges on the same `errorHandler`.

### 3. Default `errorHandler` and `HTTPException`

The module-level default in `hono-base.ts`:
```ts
const errorHandler: ErrorHandler = (err, c) => {
  if ('getResponse' in err) {
    const res = err.getResponse()
    return c.newResponse(res.body, res)
  }
  console.error(err)
  return c.text('Internal Server Error', 500)
}
```
This special-cases `HTTPException` (`src/http-exception.ts:46`, `extends Error`, with a `getResponse(): Response` method at line 66) so that throwing `new HTTPException(status, ...)` anywhere in a handler/middleware produces that exact response, while any other `Error` falls back to a generic 500.

### 404 handling
Separately, `onNotFound` (the `notFoundHandler`, default `(c) => c.text('404 Not Found', 404)`) is invoked directly inside `dispatch()` in `compose.ts` when no handler matched at index `i` and `context.finalized === false` — this is not an error/exception path, just an alternate branch of the same dispatch function.

### Not verified
- `src/compose.test.ts` and the error-handling assertions in `src/hono.test.ts` (didn't open them to confirm test expectations match this reading).
- `types.ts`'s exact `ErrorHandler`/`Next` type declarations (only inferred from usage).
- Behavior in adapters (`src/adapter/*`) for uncaught/non-`Error` throws reaching the runtime — out of scope of this trace.

